            ("What type of MOSFET possesses a doped conducting channel that conducts at zero gate voltage and requires negative voltage to turn off?", "Depletion-Mode MOSFET", ["Enhancement-Mode MOSFET", "BJT", "IGBT"], "Depletion-mode MOSFETs are normally on at VGS = 0, useful in current sources and startup circuits."),
            ("What crucial MOSFET parameter defines the minimum gate-to-source voltage required to create the conducting channel inversion layer?", "Gate Threshold Voltage (VGS(th))", ["Pinch-Off Voltage", "Breakdown Voltage", "Saturation Voltage"], "VGS must exceed threshold voltage (typically 1-3V for logic-level, 3-5V for standard power FETs) to turn the device on."),
            ("What parameter defines the internal electrical resistance between Drain and Source when a power MOSFET is fully turned on?", "Drain-Source On-Resistance (RDS(on))", ["Transconductance", "Gate Resistance", "Drain Leakage"], "Low RDS(on) (often single-digit milliohms) minimizes conduction heat losses in high-efficiency switching power supplies."),
            ("Why do power MOSFETs naturally share current equally when placed in parallel?", "As MOSFET junction temperature rises, RDS(on) increases, naturally balancing and sharing current equally", ["MOSFETs have built-in cooling fans", "Parallel MOSFETs fuse together", "MOSFETs switch to bipolar mode at high heat"], "Unlike BJTs, parallel MOSFETs self-stabilize: hotter devices develop higher resistance, shunting current to cooler parallel FETs."),
